.TH zpuzzle "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zpuzzle \- GUI fifteen puzzle
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzpuzzle [\fIoptions\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zpuzzle -- GUI fifteen puzzle
Usage:	zpuzzle [ options ]
Keys:	h/j/k/l slide a tile left/down/up/right, n new game
.HE
.B zpuzzle
is the classic fifteen puzzle: fifteen numbered tiles and one gap on
a four-by-four board.
Slide the tiles until they read 1 to 15 in order.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop and accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications; see
.BR zview .
.PP
A left click on any tile in the same row or column as the gap slides
the whole run of tiles between it and the gap one place \- so one
click can move several tiles.
Clicks elsewhere are ignored.
.PP
The keys move one tile at a time and name the direction the tile
moves:
.DS
.ta 6n 16n
	\fBh\fR	slide a tile left
	\fBl\fR	slide a tile right
	\fBk\fR	slide a tile up
	\fBj\fR	slide a tile down
	\fBn\fR	new game (also the window menu's New entry)
.DE
The status line counts moves \- each tile slid counts as one \- and
announces `Solved in N moves!' when the puzzle is done; the board
then freezes until a new game is dealt.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B zview
.SH NOTES
The deal is made by walking the gap 400 random legal moves from the
solved position, so every game is solvable \- which is true of only
half of all random tile arrangements.
The window has a fixed size (the
.B \-S
option is ignored).
